

The heart of it is deciding which tests are genuinely saying the same thing. It groups tests with similar names using Jaccard distance (the share of name-tokens two tests have in common), then within each group asks, via an R², how much of one test’s pass/fail margin can be predicted from the others in the group. A high R² means that test is redundant because its result is already implied by its neighbors. A single test is only marked removable if it has zero real failures, a comfortable margin, enough history to be trusted, and a low statistical upper bound on its true failure rate; a whole group is removed only when the group collectively clears those same bars. Before finalizing, it finds sets of tests that historically fail together and forces at least two survivors from each such set to stay, so no failure signature loses all its detectors. The output is a concrete “keep vs. cut” list with the minutes saved per module and a worst-case estimate of how many real failures the cuts would have let escape.
